<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tadukhipa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 14th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tadukhipa was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> princess, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushratta">Tushratta</a>, who was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with a rich dowry to marry the ageing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> near the end of his reign. Her marriage and dowry are recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>. After Amenhotep's death she probably joined the household of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, and some scholars have identified her with his wife Kiya.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tadukhipa.jpg" alt="Tadukhipa" width="320" height="560" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CaptMondo · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AmarnaLetterOfMarriageNegotiation-BritishMuseum-August19-08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tadukhipa, in Hurrian Tadu-Hepa, was a princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, the daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushratta">Tushratta</a> and his queen Juni, and the niece of Artashumara. Almost nothing is known of her early years. Her aunt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilukhipa">Gilukhipa</a>, Tushratta's sister, had married Pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> in the tenth year of his reign, and Tadukhipa was to marry him more than two decades later. Tushratta sent her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> around 1355 BCE, in the thirty-sixth year of Amenhotep's reign, to marry the pharaoh, who was a close ally.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushratta">Tushratta</a> sent her with many gifts: a gold-plated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> inlaid with precious stones, a pair of horses, a litter adorned with gold and precious stones, clothes, a horse saddle adorned with gold eagles, jewellery such as bracelets and armlets, and a large chest to hold them. Amenhotep had offered golden statues in return as part of the marriage agreement, but no evidence exists that they were ever sent. Tushratta had asked that his daughter become Great Royal Wife, a position already held by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiye">Tiye</a>. Tadukhipa appears in seven of Tushratta's 13 letters among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, which are the main evidence for her, and after Amenhotep's death Tushratta wrote to complain that the statues had not arrived.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> died soon after she arrived, and his son Amenhotep IV became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>. It is speculated that she then married him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushratta">Tushratta</a> wrote to him repeatedly and in one letter sent greetings to Tadu-Ḫeba, his daughter and the pharaoh's wife, together with the pharaoh's other wives, sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, horses and troops.</p><p>Some scholars tentatively identify her with Kiya, a known wife of Amenhotep IV, who lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> with her own sunshade and was shown with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> and at least one daughter. Petrie, Drioton and Vandier proposed instead that she took a new name and became Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertiti">Nefertiti</a>, whose name, &quot;the beautiful one has come&quot;, might point to foreign origin. Seele, Meyer and others reply that Tey, wife of Ay, was Nefertiti's nurse, which argues against this, since a mature princess would need no nurse, and that Nefertiti is a quintessentially Egyptian name linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tadukhipa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom sealed its alliance with Egypt through royal marriages: a daughter of Artatama I married Thutmose IV, and Shuttarna II's daughter Gilukhipa and Tushratta's daughter <b>Tadukhipa</b> were sent to Amenhotep III, which reinforced cultural exchange and confirmed Mitanni's great-power status.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
